* [`ag.quotient_curve`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Background/Algebraic-geometry/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_ag___quotient_curve_FLQQ_--statement) — Quotient curve. Declarations (LeanBridge): `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IsQuotientCurve`, `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.exists_isQuotientCurve`, `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IsQuotientCurve.surjective_left`, `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IsQuotientCurve.left_apply_eq_iff`, `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.StableAffineOpen.TwoChartCover.quotient`, `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.exists_isAffineOpen_forall_mem`. Note: Every algebraic curve over a field, proper or not, regular or not, has a quotient curve by every finite subgroup of its automorphism group (exists\_isQuotientCurve, whose only hypotheses are Finite H and IsAlgebraicCurveOver), and the projection onto any quotient curve is surjective with the H-orbits as fibres. A finite set of closed points of a curve lies in an affine open (weak approximation for a function regular on the set, a conductor at the non-normal points, then Mathlib's Zariski main theorem and prime avoidance), which gives a cover by two H-stable affine opens; the quotient is the pushout of their invariant quotients, shown to be a separated geometrically integral curve of finite type. Tau Ceti main's Quotient/FiniteGroup/Relative.lean does not cover this: it needs an H-invariant affine morphism to a base, which it does not construct. The transport lemma isLocalization\_away\_fixedPoints and finiteType\_specPreimage overlap main's isLocalization\_of\_mem\_range\_iff and finiteType\_subalgebra, which are not at the pin.
