* [`alg.bezout_matrix`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Background/Ring-theory/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_alg___bezout_matrix_FLQQ_--statement) — Bézout matrix. Declarations (LeanBridge · Mathlib): `Polynomial.bezoutMatrix`, `Polynomial.sub_mul_sum_bezoutMatrix`, `Polynomial.det_bezoutMatrix`, `Polynomial.resultant`, `Polynomial.sylvester`. Note: Formalised on the user's ruling of 2026-11-11 ("Something that has a different but fixable definition isn't partial. Something that is strictly weaker is partial."): corrected. False as written: (f(x)g(y) − f(y)g(x))/(x − y) has degree at most n − 1 in each variable, so with exponents 1 to n there are no c\_ij in general (n = 2, f = x², g = 1 gives x + y, with no term divisible by xy), and with exponents 0 to n − 1 that example has matrix (0 1; 1 0) of determinant −1 while the resultant is 1. LeanBridge formalises the corrected statements. The wording is in the LMFDB issues.
