nvariant at l (IsInvariantOf.of\_isInvariantOf: an extension with the residue degree, ramification index and Swan slopes of a member has J too). So fieldCount = Set.ncard (I.family K) is the number of extensions in I/K: natCast\_fieldCount ((fieldCount : N-infinity) = Set.encard, the true cardinality) and fieldCount\_eq\_card\_toFinset. Second blocker of the sweep (identification of the family's Swan slopes, Deligne's positive upper breaks visibleSwanSlopes, with the convex-hull Swan slopes of lf.slopes): not proved; proposed formalised on the reading that the family's 'Swan slopes' are the positive upper ramification breaks, which is how lf.slopes itself states its Swan slopes are ('The Swan slopes are the same as the positive upper ramification jumps of L/K ... For the general case where L/K is separable but not necessarily Galois see the appendix to Deligne') and how lf.swan\_slopes defines them (lf.swan\_slopes is formalised with exactly visibleSwanSlopes, round AC/ruling of 2026-11-11); the same kind of reading as the Galois reading accepted for lf.top\_slope (2026-11-11) and lf.galois\_mean\_slope (round AF). JUDGEMENT CALL for the lead: the sweep of 2026-11-06 listed this identification as a blocker of lf.family\_field\_count, lf.family\_polynomial and lf.herbrand\_function (whose only blocker it is); if the user keeps that position, lf.family\_field\_count stays partial with exactly one blocker: 'lf.slopes: for a finite separable L/K the convex-hull Swan slopes equal Deligne's positive upper breaks' (route: the discriminant exponent of a subextension E as the Artin conductor of the permutation representation on Hom\_K(E, M), i.e. Hilbert's different formula for M/K and M/E; the G\^u-orbit block systems give the vertices; every other subfield lies on or above the polygon; the lower convex hull itself is on Tau Ceti main only; estimated 1000-2000 lines), and lf.family\_mass should then carry the same blocker, since its family is the same. No text error. Review