unction\_of\_le), so d(L/K) = e - 1 + e s\_w - r\_w = e - 1 + e m\_w (tinyRam\_eq\_swanSlope\_sub\_mean), and c = f d (Tau Ceti discriminantExponent\_eq\_inertiaDegree\_mul\_differentExponent, discriminantExponent being the multiplicity of the maximal ideal of O\_K in the discriminant ideal). Checked by hand on Q\_2(sqrt 2) (c = 3), Q\_3(3\^(1/3)) (c = 5, ram 3/2) and the tame case (w = 0, c = f(e - 1)). This also proves lf.means's 'The mean m of L/K is determined by the formula c = f(e-1+em)' for m = m\_w (a side result for lf.means, whose slope-polygon definition is still its own gap). (7) Artin slopes \[s\~\_1..s\~\_w\]: the definition visibleArtinSlopes E = Swan slopes + 1, the wild Artin slopes of lf.slopes ('The Swan slopes of L/K are obtained by subtracting 1 from each Artin slope'; the LMFDB's artin2swan does the same), visibleArtinSlopes\_eq; for E/K Galois they are the committed wildArtinSlopesOfIsGalois (visibleArtinSlopes\_eq\_wildArtinSlopesOfIsGalois, added by the review). (9) Means: mean\_visibleHerbrandInvariant (the Herbrand invariant of E has the means of I). (10) Rams: visibleRams\_eq (Deligne's lower breaks of E are r\_1..r\_w), via IsInvariantOf.hasSameRams\_visibleHerbrandInvariant (committed hasSameRams\_of\_isInvariantOf + isInvariantOf\_visibleHerbrandInvariant) and map\_ram\_ofRams. The data of the family as a whole (field count, ambiguity, mass, absolute mass) are cross-references. Non-vacuity: every finite extension E has the invariant visibleHerbrandInvariant E at the residue characteristic (committed isInvariantOf\_visibleHerbrandInvariant, mk\_mem\_family\_visibleHerbrandInvariant), also over Q\_p (scratch NonVacuity.lean). Readings: the family's p is the residue characteristic of K (the knowl's and the LMFDB's setting: lf.herbrand\_invariant defines I for extensions of residue characteristic p; LeanBridge's family admits any p, and a family at another prime can be nonempty, e.g. e = 6 over Q\_3 with p = 2, where the rams of I are not 