and \[s\_1..s\_w\]\_eps\^f, as (multiset, tameDegree, inertiaDegree)). Claims: div\_eq\_pow\_slopeMultiplicity (a segment of B of slope \> 1 from (n1, c1) to (n2, c2) has n2/n1 = p\^m, m the multiplicity of its slope); mem\_hullWildSwanSlopes\_iff (the wild Swan slopes are exactly the positive Swan slopes); hullWildArtinSlopes\_eq\_visibleArtinSlopes and hullWildSwanSlopes\_eq\_visibleSwanSlopes (the wild Swan slopes, with multiplicities, are Deligne's positive upper ramification breaks of L/K, the committed visibleSwanSlopes); hullWildSwanSlopes\_eq\_swanSlopesOfIsGalois (for L/K Galois, Serre's positive upper breaks of Gal(L/K), the committed swanSlopesOfIsGalois); visibleArtinSlopeContent\_eq and visibleSwanSlopeContent\_eq (the two contents through the upper breaks). Route: the vertex theorem is proved once and shared with lf.means. (1) LowerHullVertices: if the lines of slopes tau\_t through points (n\_t, y\_t) of a set S lie below S, then the lower convex hull of S is the graph of the maximum of these lines over \[n\_0, n\_(k+1)\], with its segments, slopes and log\_p multiplicities (IsLowerHullVertices.lowerConvexHull\_eq, segmentSlopes\_lowerConvexHull, mem\_polygonEdges\_lowerConvexHull, wildSegmentSlopes\_lowerConvexHull). (2) SubgroupSlopePolygon applies it to the subgroups J of the Galois group G of the Galois closure M that contain H = Gal(M/L). The points (\[G:J\], c(J)), with c(J) = \[G:J\] sum\_i (\#G\_i - \#(J meet G\_i))/\#G\_0, have their vertices at G and at the H G\_t, with slopes tau\_t = sum\_(i\<t) \#G\_i/\#G\_0 = 1 + phi\_(M/K)(t - 1) (isLowerHullVertices\_subgroupSlopePoints, natCast\_subgroupSlope\_succ). c(J) is the discriminant exponent of M\^J/K (natCast\_discriminantExponent\_eq\_subgroupDiscriminantExponent, through the different in the tower M/M\^J/K). (3) VisibleSlopes carries this over through the Galois correspondence inside K-bar (subextensionPoints\_eq). Text error (fixable, ruling of 2026-11-11): 'The Swan slopes are the same as the positive upper ramification jumps of \$L/K\$.' is false as written whenever f \> 1 or eps \> 1. The knowl's own Swan slopes (all Artin slopes minus 1) then include -1 (the unramified segment, Artin slope 0) or 0 (the tame segment, Artin slope 1). Corrected: 'The wild Swan slopes are the same as the positive upper ramification jumps of \$L/K\$, with multiplicities.' The LMFDB's pages also use 'Swan slopes' this way, listing only the wild ones. The corrected statement is proved in full, multiplicities included, and no true claim is dropped. Bump: no longer needed. Nothing from Tau Ceti's main is used. TauCeti.lowerConvexEnvelope, IntermediateField.discriminantExponent and FiniteCompressedSlopeData.segmentSlope (the needs\_main of the the round of 28 October record) are replaced by round AJ's lowerConvexHull, segmentSlopes and polygonEdges and by the pin's discriminantExponent on finite intermediate fields. Main has no hull of subextension points to rebase on: its convex-sequence files treat envelopes of height sequences (Newton polygons). Consistent with lf.means: both are formalised through the same vertex theorem, so the tracker's medium rating no longer applies. No category field (LeanBridge declarations). Review (AM1 reviewer, 2026-11-17; review/AM1\_slopes.md): formalised, confirmed. Every sentence of the live text (2025-05-28) checked against the Lean; the vertex theorem (IsLowerHullVertices.lowerConvexHull\_eq, isLowerHullVertices\_subgroupSlopePoints, natCast\_discriminantExponent\_eq\_subgroupDiscriminantExponent, sum\_filter\_replicate\_eq\_map\_swanSlopesOfSubgroup) followed step by step; independent example Q\_3(3\^(1/6)): points (1,0), (2,1), (3,5), (6,11), Artin slopes \{1, 5/2\}, wild \[5/2\], Swan slopes \{0, 3/2\}, wild \[3/2\], Deligne's break 3/2. The text error is real (a fixable error, not a typo; it changes no outcome), but the old string as recorded above does not occur in the live text, which breaks the line after 'jumps of': the exact old string is 'The Swan slopes are the same as the positive upper ramification jumps of' followed by a line break and '\$L/K\$.' (once), the new 'The wild Swan slopes are the same as the positive upper ramification jumps of' followed by a line break and '\$L/K\$, with multiplicities.'. The bump is not needed: nothing of Tau Ceti's main is used or restated beyond two short lemmas (fold items). Style fixes only: 58 untyped local haves typed or removed, 14 headers that rebound variables, two names qualified through two namespaces, HerbrandInvariant.monotone\_swanSlope deleted (a restatement of the committed HerbrandInvariant.swanSlope\_mono), three repeated proof terms factored into helpers, layout; no statement or name of a decls entry changed. Leads for lf.visible\_slopes, lf.heights and lf.rams in the review.
