* [`nf.galois_group.name`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Fields/Number-fields/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_nf___galois_group___name_FLQQ_--statement) — Galois group of a number field -- naming convention. Declarations (Mathlib · LeanBridge · Tau Ceti): `Polynomial.Gal.galActionHom`, `Polynomial.exists_card_rootSet_eq_six_and_nonempty_gal_mulEquiv`, `Polynomial.galXPowThreeSubTwoMulEquiv`, `Polynomial.bijective_galActionHom_X_pow_three_sub_two`, `Polynomial.card_rootSet_X_pow_three_sub_two`, `TauCeti.hasGaloisLabel_X_pow_three_sub_two`, `Polynomial.galMinpolySplittingFieldXPowThreeSubTwoMulEquiv`, `Polynomial.card_rootSet_minpoly_splittingField_X_pow_three_sub_two`. Note: Round AI (AI2), reviewed. Formalised by an example in the LMFDB's setting, following the ruling of 2026-11-13 on nf.sextic\_twin: a mathematical remark is a stated fact, to be proved by an example. The 'nTt' search syntax, the short names (Cj, Dj, Sj, Aj, the alias table), what a label encodes and the search behaviour are site conventions; that a Galois group is a faithful transitive permutation group belongs to gg.galois\_group (Mathlib's galActionHom\_injective and galAction\_isPretransitive, linked there). The remark is 'An abstract group may have more than one representation as a Galois group. Correspondingly, the familiar symbol for a group may represent several Galois groups.' The Galois group of a number field is formalised as the blueprint does for nf.galois\_group: the Galois group of a defining polynomial (Mathlib's Polynomial.Gal, the automorphism group of its splitting field, the Galois closure) acting on its roots (Polynomial.Gal.galActionHom). The example is S3, the LMFDB's own (its alias S3 stands for 3T2 and its sibling representations, lmfdb/galois\_groups/transitive\_group.py), and the smallest, since no abstract group repeats among the transitive groups of degree \<= 5. (1) The cubic field Q(cbrt 2), LMFDB 3.1.108.1, defined by the irreducible X\^3 - 2: its Galois group is S3 acting on its 3 complex roots, the group 3T2. Tau Ceti's TauCeti.hasGaloisLabel\_X\_pow\_three\_sub\_two gives the label; Polynomial.card\_rootSet\_X\_pow\_three\_sub\_two (3 roots), Polynomial.bijective\_galActionHom\_X\_pow\_three\_sub\_two (the action is the full symmetric group, from faithfulness and Tau Ceti's TauCeti.natCard\_gal\_X\_pow\_three\_sub\_two = 6) and Polynomial.galXPowThreeSubTwoMulEquiv : Gal(X\^3 - 2) ≃\* Perm (Fin 3). (2) Its Galois closure L = SplittingField (X\^3 - 2), a sextic field (Polynomial.finrank\_splittingField\_X\_pow\_three\_sub\_two). For every primitive element y of L (hypothesis Q⟮y⟯ = ⊤), that is for every defining polynomial g = minpoly Q y of L: Polynomial.galMinpolySplittingFieldXPowThreeSubTwoMulEquiv : Gal(g) ≃\* Perm (Fin 3), through Tau Ceti's TauCeti.galEquivNormalClosure (Gal(g) is the automorphism group of the normal closure of Q(y) = L, which is Gal(X\^3 - 2) by definition), and Polynomial.card\_rootSet\_minpoly\_splittingField\_X\_pow\_three\_sub\_two (Gal(g) acts on 6 complex roots, the group 6T2). (3) Added by the review, the remark as one unconditional statement, Polynomial.exists\_card\_rootSet\_eq\_six\_and\_nonempty\_gal\_mulEquiv: there is an irreducible g in Q\[X\] with 6 complex roots and Gal(g) ≃\* Perm (Fin 3) (a primitive element exists by Mathlib's Field.exists\_primitive\_element). So the abstract group S3 is the Galois group of a cubic and of a sextic number field, with faithful transitive permutation representations of degrees 3 and 6, which differ. The label 6T2 itself is not formalised (T-numbers in degree \>= 6 are gg.tnumber's gap, issue \#49); the remark does not need it, the degrees already telling the two representations apart. Doubtful reading, flagged by the audit: a minor 'may' remark inside a search knowl, formalised on the ruling above. No error of mathematics in the text; two pure typos recorded (a missing word 'representation', a missing comma). Review: three declarations renamed after