* [`group.complete`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Abstract-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_group___complete_FLQQ_--statement) — Complete group. Declarations (LeanBridge · Mathlib): `Group.IsComplete`, `MulAut.conj`, `Group.isComplete_iff`, `Group.isComplete_iff_forall_exists_isComplement'`, `Subgroup.isComplement'_centralizer`, `Equiv.Perm.isComplete`, `Equiv.Perm.surjective_conj_of_card_ne_six`, `Equiv.Perm.center_eq_bot`. Note: Round AG. Both recorded gaps are closed. (1) Baer's theorem, Group.isComplete\_iff\_forall\_exists\_isComplement': G is complete iff for every group H of the universe of G and every injective f : G →\* H with normal image, f.range has a normal complement. The forward direction holds for H in any universe, with the centralizer of f.range as the normal complement (Group.IsComplete.isComplement'\_centralizer\_range); the converse only needs groups of G's own universe, the strongest form. The new part is the centre half (center\_eq\_bot\_of\_forall\_exists\_isComplement'): a central z is identified with the central commutator c = (0,0,1) = \[(1,0,0),(0,1,0)\], of infinite order, of Tau Ceti's Heisenberg group H(Z) in the central product (G x H(Z))/\<(z, c\^-1)\>, in which G is normal; a retraction onto G along a normal complement sends H(Z), which centralizes G, into Z(G), hence the commutator c, which equals z, to 1. The automorphism half (surjective\_conj\_of\_forall\_exists\_isComplement') applies the existing holomorph argument (G ⋊ Aut(G), in G's universe). (2) S\_n is complete for n != 2, 6 (Equiv.Perm.isComplete, for every finite type, with Nat.card): Z(S\_n) = 1 for n \>= 3 (center\_eq\_bot), S\_0 and S\_1 are trivial, and every automorphism is inner for n != 6 (surjective\_conj\_of\_card\_ne\_six; the shared theorem also used for nf.sextic\_twin): an automorphism keeps centralizer orders, and 2\^k k! (n-2k)! = 2 (n-2)! forces k = 1 unless (n, k) = (6, 3), so transpositions go to transpositions (isSwap\_mulAut\_apply); an automorphism sending transpositions to transpositions is conjugation by x -\> phi(a x)(z) (mem\_range\_conj\_of\_forall\_isSwap; for n \<= 2 every automorphism is the identity). The second condition is the recorded LMFDB error ('inner automorphism group' should read 'automorphism group'; G -\> Inn(G) is always onto with kernel Z(G)), a fixable text under the ruling of 2026-11-11; corrected, it is the definition IsComplete (MulAut.conj bijective). Review (AG2): formalised, confirmed; every sentence of the corrected text is proved, and the centraliser-order count, the small cases and Baer's centre half were checked by hand. The forward direction now states its witness, the centralizer (it was IsComplete.exists\_normal\_isComplement'), as the house rule on existentials asks; the class docstring of Group.IsComplete in committed GroupTheory/Complete.lean, which still says that Baer's converse is not proved for the centre, is stale (recorded in review/AG2\_symmetric.md). Axioms: propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.
* [`group.computing_subgroup_labels`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Abstract-groups/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_group___computing_subgroup_labels_FLQQ_--statement) — Computing labels of subgroups. Declarations (LeanBridge): `Subgroup.IsGassmannEquivalent`, `Subgroup.IsAutGassmannEquivalent`, `Subgroup.natCard_inter_eq_sum`, `Subgroup.mk_le_mk_iff_exists_smul_le`, `TauCeti.AffineGroup.Ioi_mk_map_inl_ne`, `Equiv.Perm.Ioi_mk_range_kleinFourOnFourHom_ne`, `DihedralGroup.isConjugate_zpowers_sr_zero_zpowers_sr_two`, `DihedralGroup.not_isConjugate_subgroupOf_centralizer`. Note: Round AI (AI1), from the Nothing-to-formalise audit (N2: To be formalised, medium); formalised, confirmed in review after the automorphism variant of the remark was added. The labelling algorithm (the orderings, lex order on label lists, permutation-representation signatures and where they are computed, efficiency) is a database convention; its mathematical content is proved. (1) 'a unique identifier for each Gassmann class: we count the intersection of H with each G-conjugacy class': LeanBridge's Subgroup.IsGassmannEquivalent is exactly equality of these counts (the definition of group.gassmann\_equivalence). The procedure that computes them, 'enumerate H-conjugacy classes ..., determine which G-conjugacy class they belong to, adding the size of the H-conjugacy class to a running tally': Subgroup.natCard\_inter\_eq\_sum: \|C cap H\| = sum of \|D\| over the conjugacy classes D of H with ConjClasses.map H.subtype D = C (each H-class lies in one G-class, its image). (2) 'An analogous process works for Gassmann vectors up to automorphism, where we collect together G-conjugacy classes that are related by an automorphism of G': LeanBridge's Subgroup.IsAutGassmannEquivalent (counts on the orbits of Aut(G) on G), each orbit a disjoint union of conjugacy classes (MulAut.orbit\_eq\_iUnion\_carrier, MulAut.pairwiseDisjoint\_carrier\_orbit); Subgroup.natCard\_orbit\_inter\_eq\_finsum: the count on an orbit is the sum of the counts on the classes it collects; added in review, Subgroup.IsGassmannEquivalent.isAutGassmannEquivalent (finite G). (3) 'supergroup refers to inclusions in the poset of subgroups up to automorphism (resp., conjugation) ... K is a supergroup of H if it contains any subgroup equivalent to H': the order of SubgroupClassPoset.lean (Mathlib's quotient preorder, see group.subgroup.diagram.lmfdb) with A = MulAut G resp. ConjAct G, Subgroup.mk\_le\_mk\_iff\_exists\_smul\_le, a partial order for finite G (so 'poset' holds); the proper supergroups of \[H\] are Set.Ioi \[H\] (Subgroup.mk\_lt\_mk\_iff\_exists\_smul\_le). (4) '(One can and often does have Gassmann equivalent subgroups for which the lists of supergroups differ.)': a stated fact, proved by an example in the LMFDB's setting (ruling of 2026-11-13; 'often' is a frequency remark, as group.rank's 'often smaller'), and the review decided that it needs one example for each variant, the paragraph treating 'up to automorphism (resp., conjugation)' in parallel and neither case implying the other. Up to conjugacy (the author's): in Hol(C\_8) x C\_2, with Hol(C\_8) = Tau Ceti's AffineGroup (ZMod 8) (LMFDB 32.43), H0 = \{(0,a)\} (affineLinearSubgroup) and K0 = \{(c(a),a)\}, c(3) = c(5) = 4, c(1) = c(7) = 0 (zmodEightTwistedSubgroup): H0 x 1 and K0 x 1 are Gassmann equivalent (isGassmannEquivalent\_map\_inl; (c(a),a) is conjugate to (0,a) by a translation, via Subgroup.isGassmannEquivalent\_range) and Set.Ioi \[H0 x 1\] != Set.Ioi \[K0 x 1\] among the conjugacy classes (Ioi\_mk\_map\_inl\_ne): \[H0 x C\_2\] lies strictly above \[H0 x 1\] but not above \[K0 x 1\] (no conjugate of K0 lies in H0, the stabiliser of 0, as (4,3) and (4,5) have no common fixed point). These two subgroups are automorphic (by (b,a) -\> (b + c(a), a); checked by brute force, not formalised), which is the LMFDB's situation for the part j of a label but makes them one subgroup up to automorphism, so the example says nothing about that variant. Up to automorphism (added in review): in S\_7 = Perm (Fin 7), the Klein four-groups V1 = \<(0 1)(2 3), (0 2)(1 3)\> and V2 = \<(0 1)(2 3), (0 1)(4 5)\> (Equiv.Perm.kleinFourOnFourHom, kleinFourOnSixHom) are Gassmann equivalent (isGassmannEquivalent\_range\_kleinFourOnFourHom), hence Gassmann equivalent up to automorphism (isAutGassmannEquivalent\_range\_kleinFourOnFourHom), and Set.Ioi \[V1\] != Set.Ioi \[V2\] in orbitRel.Quotient (MulAut S\_7) (Subgroup S\_7) (Equiv.Perm.Ioi\_mk\_range\_kleinFourOnFourHom\_ne): the subgroup M fixing 5 and 6 contains V1, no automorphism maps M into V1 (M has an element of order 3), and no automorphism maps V2 into M, every automorphism of S\_7 being inner (the committed Equiv.Perm.surjective\_conj\_of\_card\_ne\_six) and V2 fixing only the point 6. Since labels identify classes, the sorted label lists differ. (5) 'there may be two or more G-conjugates of H contained in K that are not K-conjugate': in D\_4 (Mathlib's DihedralGroup 4, LMFDB 8.3), \<s\> and \<sr\^2\> are conjugate (DihedralGroup.isConjugate\_zpowers\_sr\_zero\_zpowers\_sr\_two, by r), both lie in K = centralizer \{s\} = \{1, r\^2, s, sr\^2\} (zpowers\_sr\_zero\_le\_centralizer, added in review, and zpowers\_sr\_two\_le\_centralizer), and they are not conjugate in K (DihedralGroup.not\_isConjugate\_subgroupOf\_centralizer), using LeanBridge's Subgroup.IsConjugate in the group K. 'We can, in fact, compute with any supergroup K' and 'much more efficient' are about the computation of signatures, which the knowl does not define. Not linked for want of room: natCard\_orbit\_inter\_eq\_finsum, IsGassmannEquivalent.isAutGassmannEquivalent, mk\_lt\_mk\_iff\_exists\_smul\_le, isGassmannEquivalent\_map\_inl and isAutGassmannEquivalent\_range\_kleinFourOnFourHom (both named in the docstrings of the linked Ioi theorems), the two containments in D\_4. Text: 'Gassman vectors' is a pure typo (already recorded). Review changes: the S\_7 example and the general lemmas