ilies above). Review (AG1): formalised, confirmed. IsAlmostSimple is the LMFDB's definition (group.almost\_simple: S \<= G \<= Aut(S) for a non-abelian simple S, as an embedding G -\> Aut(S) whose image contains Inn(S)); the edge cases are excluded exactly (n = 1: PGL(1, q) is trivial; PGL(2, 2) and PGL(2, 3) have no non-abelian simple normal subgroup) and checked on PGL(3, 2) (simple) and PGL(2, 5) (almost simple, not simple). The user's ruling is met by PGL(n, q) itself, which is trivially a closed subgroup of PGL(n, q), so the closedness of the image of PSL(n, q) (true over a finite field, not formalised) is not needed; PSL(n, q) now sits inside PGL(n, q) as a normal subgroup with trivial centraliser, the point round AF raised. The closing clause 'and so they play an important role in the classification of finite simple groups' draws a consequence and states no theorem beyond the simplicity results; the classification itself is not invoked. Review changes: proofs use Mathlib's le\_centralizer\_iff\_isMulCommutative and MulAut.conjNormal\_val instead of hand-made copies; the repeated cancellation in eq\_one\_of\_mul\_mul\_scalar\_eq is one local lemma; layout, a hoisted n, and docstrings that no longer assert PGL(2, 2) = S3, PGL(2, 3) = S4 or the quotient PGL/PSL, which are not proved.

