# Groups › Lattices

* [`lattice.algebraic`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Lattices/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lattice___algebraic_FLQQ_--statement) — Algebraic lattice. Declarations (LeanBridge): `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.IsAlgebraic`, `NumberField.minkowskiForm`, `NumberField.minkowskiForm_eq_sum`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.IsAlgebraic.isPosDef`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.isAlgebraic_unitLattice_unit`. Note: Formalised on the user's ruling of 2026-11-11 ("Something that has a different but fixable definition isn't partial. Something that is strictly weaker is partial."): reading. The knowl fixes neither the bilinear form on the number field nor the rank of the module. IsAlgebraic takes the Minkowski form and full rank. With twisted trace forms Tr(alpha x y), every \<a\> with a \> 0 is algebraic. Even with the Minkowski form, \<2\> is algebraic as Z.1 in Q(sqrt 2) once full rank is dropped, yet IsAlgebraic excludes it. The wording is in the LMFDB issues.
* [`lattice.arithmetic`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Lattices/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lattice___arithmetic_FLQQ_--statement) — Arithmetic lattice. Declarations (LeanBridge): `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsArithmeticLattice`, `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsIntegralLattice`, `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsSimilarLattice`, `LinearMap.BilinForm.isArithmeticLattice_iff`, `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsSimilarLattice.isArithmeticLattice`, `LinearMap.BilinForm.isArithmeticLattice_of_apply_self_eq`. Note: Both gaps are closed. Arithmeticity is now defined for a Z-submodule L of any real space with a bilinear form B, indefinite ones included (IsArithmeticLattice: c B(L, L) in Z for some real c \> 0), with the knowl's notions beside it: integral (B(L, L) in Z) and similar (L isometric to alpha M for a real alpha /= 0, by a Z-linear bijection preserving the forms). Proved: a lattice similar to an integral lattice in any real quadratic space is arithmetic (c = alpha\^-2), and an arithmetic L is similar to the integral lattice sqrt(c) L in the same space (L = sqrt(c)\^-1 (sqrt(c) L)), which is finitely generated and discrete when L is (Submodule.FG.pointwise\_smul, Submodule.discreteTopology\_pointwise\_smul); so isArithmeticLattice\_iff: arithmetic iff similar to an integral lattice. In a real inner product space it is LeanBridge's ZLattice.IsArithmetic (isArithmeticLattice\_innerₗ\_iff). The forms need not be symmetric or nondegenerate for any of this. Review: isArithmeticLattice\_iff now states, for a finitely generated discrete L (the knowl's lattice), that the integral lattice K is itself finitely generated and discrete (before, that was only an unstated composite of FG.pointwise\_smul and discreteTopology\_pointwise\_smul with an iff over arbitrary integral submodules). And since lattice.isometry, read literally (a recorded text error there), allows any map preserving Q, and lattice.similarity allows alpha = 0, isArithmeticLattice\_of\_apply\_self\_eq proves that a lattice with a Q-preserving map, linear or not, into alpha M (any real alpha, M integral, B symmetric) is arithmetic, by polarisation: the arithmetic lattices are the same on the literal and the corrected reading of 'similar', so the knowl does not inherit lattice.isometry's text error.
* [`lattice.conway_genus_symbol`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Lattices/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lattice___conway_genus_symbol_FLQQ_--statement) — Conway symbol. Declarations (LeanBridge): `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.conwayGenusSymbolSigned`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.SameGenus.conwayGenusSymbolSigned_eq`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.compartmentOddities`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.trainSigns`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.SameGenus.compartmentOddities_eq`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.SameGenus.trainSigns_eq`. Note: Every component of the Conway genus symbol is now defined and proved to be a genus invariant with no hypothesis: the type, signature, Jordan ranks at every prime, signs at the odd primes and dyadic types (conwayGenusSymbolSigned), and the train signs and compartment oddities of the canonical 2-adic symbol (Sage's canonical reduction), the latter through the round of 1 November's Gauss oddities Theta\_m, now genus invariants (SameGenus.dyadicGaussOddity\_eq) by the evaluation sqrt2\^((k+1)n) eps\^(k-1) zeta\_8\^t (k \>= 2) of the dyadic Gauss sums of unimodular Z\_2-forms and their factorisation over a Jordan splitting. That is all the knowl defines and claims (an invariant of the genus of the form I\_\{r,s\}(...) or II\_\{r,s\}(...), details in Conway-Sloane ch. 15), so the outcome is formalised. Not formalised, and not part of the definition: the converse at 2 (the symbol and the signature determine the dyadic localization; the odd-prime half is sameGenus\_iff\_conwayGenusSymbolSigned\_eq).
* [`lattice.definition`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Lattices/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lattice___definition_FLQQ_--statement) — Integral Lattice. Declarations (LeanBridge): `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsQuadraticSpace`, `LinearMap.BilinForm.gramPolynomial`, `Submodule.IsDiscreteLattice`, `LinearMap.BilinForm.IsIntegralLattice`, `LinearMap.BilinForm.ratModelIsometry`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.realisationIsometry`. Note: The gap is closed. The knowl's objects are now formalised directly: IsQuadraticSpace (V with a nonsingular symmetric B, Q = BilinMap.toQuadraticMap B); gramPolynomial, the quadratic form sum\_ij B(v\_i, v\_j) x\_i x\_j of B in a basis, homogeneous of degree 2 (isHomogeneous\_gramPolynomial) with value Q(sum x\_i v\_i) (eval\_gramPolynomial); Submodule.IsDiscreteLattice, an R-lattice (finitely generated R-submodule that is discrete in V, for any ring R acting on V, in particular a subring of the topological field F), a lattice being the case R = Z over F = R, integral when B(L, L) ⊆ Z (ZY's IsIntegralLattice); the example: (R\^n, ·) is a quadratic space (Matrix.isQuadraticSpace\_toBilin'\_one) and Z\^n a discrete integral lattice in it (Matrix.isDiscreteLattice\_span\_basisFun, Matrix.isIntegralLattice\_span\_basisFun). The bridge to Tau Ceti, for every signature and degenerate lattices included: ratModel/ratModelIsometry send an integral lattice of a real quadratic space to Tau Ceti's rationalisation (ofIntegralForm) with isometric integral form; realisationIsometry proves every TauCeti.IntegralLattice isometric to the rational model of the discrete integral lattice Z\^ι ⊕ 0 of R\^(ι ⊕ ι) with the block form \[\[G, 1\], \[1, 0\]\] (G a Gram matrix), nonsingular even for singular G (Matrix.isQuadraticSpace\_gramRealForm). The topology of V is a parameter (the knowl's natural topology of V over F; Fin n -\> R in the example). 'Currently, all the lattices in the database are nondegenerate, integral, and have signature (n\_+, n\_-) with n\_+ \>= n\_-' is a statement about the database, not mathematics. No text error found.
* [`lattice.density`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Lattices/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lattice___density_FLQQ_--statement) — Density of an integral lattice. Declarations (LeanBridge): `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.density`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.centerDensity`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.latticePackingDensity`, `EuclideanSpace.spherePackingDensity`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.packingDensity_range_eq_density`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.tendsto_volume_iUnion_ball_inter_ball_div`, `EuclideanSpace.unitBallVolume_two_mul_add_one`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.latticePackingDensity_le_spherePackingDensity`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.IsDensestLattice`. Note: Formalised on the user's ruling of 2026-11-11 ("Something that has a different but fixable definition isn't partial. Something that is strictly weaker is partial."), with the missing claim proved in round AC: corrected. 'Minimum 2ρ' must read minimum 4ρ² (ρ half the minimal length), and V\_n is the volume of the unit ball. The missing claim, that Δ is the density of the lattice-centred sphere packing, is proved: for every realisation of a positive definite lattice of positive rank, the proportion of B(0, R) covered by the balls B(v, ρ) tends to Δ (tendsto\_volume\_iUnion\_ball\_inter\_ball\_div, from Mathlib's lattice-point counting), so the packing density is Δ (packingDensity\_range\_eq\_density); the knowl's two formulas for V\_n are proved (unitBallVolume\_two\_mul, unitBallVolume\_two\_mul\_add\_one), and lattice packings are at most as dense as sphere packings. The wording is in the LMFDB issues.
* [`lattice.discriminant`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Groups/Lattices/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_lattice___discriminant_FLQQ_--statement) — Discriminant of an integral lattice. Declarations (Tau Ceti · LeanBridge): `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.determinant`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.wittDiscriminant`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.halfDiscriminant`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.wittDiscriminant_orthogonalSum_hyperbolicPlane`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.nonempty_center_even_algEquiv_adjoinRoot`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.nonempty_center_algEquiv_adjoinRoot`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.etale_adjoinRoot_wittDiscriminant`, `TauCeti.IntegralLattice.twoAdicUnitDiscriminant_eq_iff`. Note: Round AL (AL4\_clifford\_discriminant). The one gap left by ZZ4 (tracker v59 bk: 'the knowl's Clifford sentence (the centre of the even Clifford algebra in even rank, of the full one in odd rank, for nondegenerate lattices; recorded in the LMFDB issues) and its proof, with Clifford algebras of quadratic forms over Q') is closed, in the corrected form, for every nondegenerate integral lattice (Tau Ceti's IntegralLattice, any signature). Reading, the LMFDB's own: the Clifford algebra of the rational quadratic space L (x) Q with the LMFDB's quadratic form Q(v) = \<v, v\> (lattice.quadratic\_form: 'the function on L (x) Q defined by Q(v) = \<v,v\> = v\^T G v'; lattice.definition: Q(v) := B(v,v)), i.e. Tau Ceti's L.norm = L.form.toQuadraticMap on the ambient space V of L, which is L (x) Q by Tau Ceti's rationalizationIsometry. Mathlib's Clifford relation is iota(v)\^2 = L.norm v = \<v,v\>, and L.norm.discr b is the determinant of (\<b\_i, b\_j\>), which is D in a basis of L (discr\_norm\_rationalBasis), so the Witt discriminant is (-1)\^(n choose 2) times the Gram determinant of the same form (intCast\_wittDiscriminant). The convention matters in odd rank only: halving Q multiplies the discriminant by 2\^(-n), twice a square for odd n, and would make the odd-rank centre Q(sqrt(2 delta)); so Q(v) = \<v,v\> is the only convention under which the sentence holds, and it is the LMFDB's, and Lam's as Tau Ceti's centre files cite him (Chapter V, §2: z\^2 = (-1)\^(n choose 2) Q(v\_1)...Q(v\_n) with iota(v)\^2 = Q(v), Q(v) = B(v,v); the books themselves were not re-read in review). Proved: (1) TauCeti.IntegralLattice.nonempty\_center\_even\_algEquiv\_adjoinRoot: positive even rank, center Q (CliffordAlgebra.even L.norm) is isomorphic as a Q-algebra to AdjoinRoot (X\^2 - C delta), delta = wittDiscriminant L; (2) TauCeti.IntegralLattice.nonempty\_center\_algEquiv\_adjoinRoot: odd rank, the same for the centre of the full Clifford algebra; (3) etale\_adjoinRoot\_wittDiscriminant: Q\[X\]/(X\^2 - delta) is etale over Q (delta != 0, wittDiscriminant\_ne\_zero; Tau Ceti's Polynomial.Monic.isStandardEtale\_adjoinRoot), and it has rank 2 (AdjoinRoot.finrank\_X\_pow\_sub\_C), so it is the quadratic etale algebra generated by a square root of delta (the class of X). Route: the general file CenterDiscriminant.lean proves, for a nondegenerate quadratic form Q over a field with 2 invertible and any basis b, that the centre of C(Q) in odd dimension (CliffordAlgebra.nonempty\_center\_algEquiv\_adjoinRoot) and of C\_0(Q) in even positive dimension (CliffordAlgebra.nonempty\_center\_even\_algEquiv\_adjoinRoot) is K\[X\]/(X\^2 - (-1)\^(n choose 2) disc\_b(Q)), from QuadraticForm.discr\_eq\_det\_sq\_mul\_discr (Gram determinants in two bases differ by (b.det w)\^2), discr\_reindex, discr\_eq\_prod\_of\_isOrtho (diagonal Gram matrix of an orthogonal basis), AdjoinRoot.xPowSubCMulPowEquiv (K\[X\]/(X\^n - a) = K\[X\]/(X\^n - c\^n a), c != 0), Tau Ceti's adjoinRootEquivCenterEven (even) and the new odd-dimensional analogue CliffordAlgebra.adjoinRootEquivCenter (root -\> volume element v1...vn of an anisotropic orthogonal spanning list of odd length; surjective by Tau Ceti's mem\_center\_iff\_exists\_eq\_add\_smul\_volume, injective by the dimension count with Tau Ceti's finrank\_center\_eq\_two\_of\_odd\_finrank). Also proved: discr\_inv\_two\_smul\_norm\_rationalBasis (the Gram determinant of Q/2 in a basis of L is the quadratic discriminant 2\^(-n) D: the knowl's 'Doing so leads to the extra factor of 2\^\{-n\}', not flagged before), and not\_nonempty\_center\_even\_algEquiv\_adjoinRoot (in rank 0 the centre of C\_0 = Q, of dimension 1, is not Q\[X\]/(X\^2 - delta), of dimension 2: the corrected sentence needs positive rank). Non-vacuity (review scratch NonVacuity.lean): Tau Ceti's hyperbolicPlane (rank 2, delta = 1) for (1), unitLattice (Fin 1) (delta = 1) and unitLattice (Fin 3) (delta = -1) for (2), hyperbolicPlane and unitLattice (Fin 3) for (3), unitLattice (Fin 0) (nondegenerate, rank 0, delta = 1) for the rank-0 statement. With ZZ4's, T7's and Tau Ceti's declarations every stated fact is proved: D for any basis (Tau Ceti's determinant, determinant\_eq\_gramDet), U has Witt discriminant 1 (wittDiscriminant\_hyperbolicPlane), stability under adding U (wittDiscriminant\_orthogonalSum\_hyperbolicPlane), \|D\| (Tau Ceti's discriminant), the geometric discriminant as the covolume of every realisation in a real inner product space (Tau Ceti's covolume\_range\_eq\_sqrt\_discriminant; geometricDiscriminant is sqrt(discriminant) by definition), the factor 2\^(-n), the 2-adic square class (twoAdicUnitDiscriminant\_eq\_iff); the remaining sentences are bibliography or remarks ('removes the sign ambiguity completely', 'better behavior upon reduction to characteristic 2', 'used in the 2-adic classification'). Text errors to record (each old string occurs once in the live text): (a) fixable, refining and superseding ZZ4's recorded correction, whose new text is false in rank 0: old 'using this definition, the center of the even Clifford algebra associated to the lattice will be the quadratic étale algebra generated by the square root of the Witt discriminant'; new 'using this definition, for \$L\$ nondegenerate of positive rank \$n\$, the center of the even Clif