# Modular forms › Miscellaneous

* [`hecke_algebra.definition`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Modular-forms/Modular-forms-miscellaneous/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_hecke_algebra___definition_FLQQ_--statement) — Hecke algebra. Declarations (LeanBridge): `Module.End.heckeAlgebra`, `HeckeRing.GL2.cuspFormHeckeAlgebra`, `HeckeRing.GL2.modularFormHeckeAlgebra`, `HeckeRing.GL2.cuspFormCharSpaceHeckeAlgebra`, `HeckeRing.GL2.modFormCharSpaceHeckeAlgebra`, `TauCeti.ModularSymbols.heckeAlgebra`. GitHub issues: [#36](https://github.com/CBirkbeck/LeanBridge/issues/36). Note: The R-Hecke algebra of an R-module with Hecke operators as Algebra.adjoin R of the operators in End\_R(M) (commutative for commuting operators, finite over a noetherian R for finite M), instantiated over ℂ on M\_k(Γ₁(N)) and S\_k(Γ₁(N)) (all T\_n, including U\_p, and the diamonds; finite-dimensional), on M\_k(N, χ) and S\_k(N, χ) (classical T\_n of the Γ₀(N) Hecke ring; commutative), and over every commutative ring R (ℚ, finite fields, ℤ) on the modular symbols 𝕄\_w(Γ₁(N); R), equal over ℤ to Tau Ceti's heckeTAlgebra. Tau Ceti's T\_n on Γ₁(N) is the single double coset Γ₁(N) diag(1, n) Γ₁(N), which differs from the classical T\_n when p² ∣ n for a prime p ∤ N (T(1, p²) = T\_\{p²\} - p\^\{k-2\}⟨p⟩), so the diamonds are included as generators and the algebra is the classical ℂ\[T\_n, ⟨d⟩\]. Spaces of forms are taken over ℂ only, as they carry no ℚ-structure in the pinned libraries.
* [`mf.gl2.history.elliptic`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Modular-forms/Modular-forms-miscellaneous/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_mf___gl2___history___elliptic_FLQQ_--statement) — Elliptic functions. Declarations (Mathlib · LeanBridge · Tau Ceti): `ModularForm.E`, `ModularForm.eisensteinF`, `ModularForm.eisensteinF_eq`, `ModularForm.tsum_eisSummand_eq_two_mul_riemannZeta_mul_E`, `ModularForm.isQuasiModularForm_E2`, `ModularForm.discriminant`, `TauCeti.ModularForm.j`, `TauCeti.ModularForm.j_smul`. Note: Round AI (AI5\_mf\_history), reviewed (review/AI5\_mf\_history.md). Audit record N4.json: 'Partially in LeanBridge', easy; it reads the adjectives 'quasi-modular' (E\_2) and 'elliptic modular invariant' (j) as stated facts. AUDIT ERROR: the audit says j = E\_4\^3/Delta is defined in no tree; Tau Ceti has TauCeti.ModularForm.j with its invariance j\_smul at the pin. READING (the fuller reading of the handoff, taken on the coordinator's instruction): the knowl defines neither F\_k nor G\_k. G\_k is read as the sum over the lattice Z tau + Z, sum over (m, n) != (0, 0) of (m tau + n)\^-k, and F\_k as the normalisation with first Fourier coefficient 1, -B\_k/(2k) + sum sigma\_\{k-1\}(n) q\^n (Cohen-Stromberg's F\_k). Every stated fact is proved at the pins. (1) E\_k: Mathlib's ModularForm.E (the LMFDB's own link in cmf.eisenstein). (2) 'their alternately normalized counterparts F\_k and G\_k': ModularForm.eisensteinF with eisensteinF\_eq, F\_k = -(B\_k/(2k)) E\_k for even k \>= 4 (Mathlib's q\_expansion\_bernoulli; B\_k != 0), and ModularForm.tsum\_eisSummand\_eq\_two\_mul\_riemannZeta\_mul\_E, G\_k = 2 zeta(k) E\_k for k \>= 3 (the composite of Mathlib's tsum\_eisSummand\_eq\_riemannZeta\_mul\_eisensteinSeries with the definition of E; the term of (0, 0) in the sum over Z\^2 is 0). (3) 'quasi-modular' E\_2: LeanBridge's isQuasiModularForm\_E2 (depth exactly 1: hasQuasiModularDepth\_E2). (4) Delta: Mathlib's ModularForm.discriminant. (5) 'elliptic modular invariant'