* [`mf.gl2.history.new`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Modular-forms/Modular-forms-miscellaneous/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_mf___gl2___history___new_FLQQ_--statement) — New modular forms from given ones. Declarations (LeanBridge): `ModularForm.multiplierSpace`, `ModularForm.derivOfComplex_smul`, `ModularForm.isQuasiModularForm_derivOfComplex`, `ModularForm.rankinCohenBracket`, `ModularForm.rankinCohenBracket_mem_multiplierSpace`, `ModularForm.qExpansion_twistOfWidth_coeff`, `ModularForm.sum_slash_eq_trace`, `HeckeRing.GL2.heckeTNat_apply_of_level_one`. Note: Round AI follow-up (AI9c\_rankin\_cohen). This record overrides the record of AI9b\_history\_new for this knowl (from 'Nothing to formalise'; audit N4: formalisable, medium, 'Partially in LeanBridge'). Reviewed on 2026-11-13: review/AI9b\_history\_new.md for DerivativeLaw.lean and ChangeOfGroup.lean, review/AI9c\_rankin\_cohen.md for the two RankinCohen.lean files (written by the first reviewer, checked by a second, independent one); this record is the second review's, and sections 1, 3 and 4 below are taken from the first review's record. OUTCOME: formalised. Every mathematical sentence of the knowl is proved, three of them after a text correction (TEXT ERRORS 1 to 3 below, fixable errors), in four files: Multiplier/DerivativeLaw.lean (437 lines), Multiplier/ChangeOfGroup.lean (492 lines), RingTheory/Derivation/RankinCohen.lean (177 lines) and Multiplier/RankinCohen.lean (239 lines). AI9b's record was partial pending a question on sentence (8), the Rankin-Cohen sentence (does it only name the family, or assert that the family sends modular forms to modular forms?). Cohen's theorem is now proved for every n, so the knowl is formalised under either reading and the question needs no answer. Setting: the modular forms of weight k in Z and multiplier system v on a subgroup Gamma of SL(2, R) are LeanBridge's ModularForm.multiplierSpace k v (holomorphic, f\|\_k gamma = v(gamma) f, bounded at every cusp); for v = 1 they are Mathlib's ModularForm Gamma k (mem\_multiplierSpace\_one\_iff). f' is ModularForm.derivOfComplex f, the derivative on H (Mathlib's D f is f'/(2 pi i)). SECTION 1, vector space and graded ring. (1) 'lambda f + mu g': multiplierSpace k v is a Submodule over C (existing), for every subgroup of SL(2, R) and every homomorphism v to the unit circle. (2) 'graded ring' for trivial multiplier: Mathlib's ModularForm.instGCommRing (every subgroup of GL(2, R) with determinants 1 or -1). (3) Product: mul\_mem\_multiplierSpace, f g is in multiplierSpace (k + l) (v \* w); it is also the case n = 0 of Cohen's theorem below (rankinCohenBracket\_zero: \[f, g\]\_0 = f g). SECTION 2, differentiation. (4) The modular identity f(gamma z) = v(gamma)(cz+d)\^k f(z) is apply\_smul\_of\_mem\_multiplierSpace; (gamma z)' = (cz+d)\^-2 for det gamma = 1 is hasDerivAt\_smul\_coe; the law is derivOfComplex\_smul, f'(gamma z) = v(gamma)((cz+d)\^\{k+2\} f'(z) + k c (cz+d)\^\{k+1\} f(z)). TEXT ERROR 1: the factor c is missing. Proved: eq\_zero\_of\_derivOfComplex\_smul, the LMFDB's formula at gamma and z forces k = 0 or c = 1 or f(z) = 0, so it fails already for translations. Old: '\\\[f'(\\gamma z)=v(\\gamma)((cz+d)\^\{k+2\}f'(z)+k(cz+d)\^\{k+1\}f(z))\\;.\\\]'; new: '\\\[f'(\\gamma z)=v(\\gamma)((cz+d)\^\{k+2\}f'(z)+kc(cz+d)\^\{k+1\}f(z))\\;.\\\]'. (5) 'f' is a quasi-modular form of weight k+2': isQuasiModularForm\_derivOfComplex (LeanBridge's IsQuasiModularForm Gamma (k+2) 1 v, depth at most 1, P(z, X) = f'(z) + k f(z) X, with holomorphy and boundedness at every cusp); 'exactly modular if k = 0': derivOfComplex\_mem\_multiplierSpace (a modular form of weight 2 in the full sense). The tool is derivOfComplex\_slash, (f\|\_k gamma)' = f'\|\_\{k+2\} gamma - k (c/(cz+d)) f\|\_k gamma for det gamma = 1, from Mathlib's normalizedDerivOfComplex\_slash. (6) The two repairs. By 1/y: ModularForm.maassShimura k f = f' + k f/(2 i y), and maassShimura\_slash (the transformation law of weight k+2 and multiplier v; the function is not holomorphic). By E\_2: serreDerivative\_mem\_multiplierSpace, Mathlib's Serre derivative D f - (k/12) E\_2 f is in multiplierSpace (k+2) v for Gamma inside SL(2, Z), the groups on which E\_2 is quasi-modular (Mathlib's serreDerivativeMF is the case v = 1). (7) Combinations: zpow\_div\_zpow\_slash (f\^l/g\^k satisfies the transformation law of weight 0 and multiplier v\^l/w\^k; integer powers, no hypothesis on zeros: the quotient is only meromorphic, so the law is all the sentence can mean); derivOfComplex\_slash\_of\_slash\_eq and derivOfComplex\_zpow\_div\_zpow\_slash ('its derivative is really modular of weight 2': the transformation law of weight 2, with no differentiability hypothesis); derivOfComplex\_zpow\_div\_zpow ('expanding': (f\^l/g\^k)' = f\^\{l-1\} g\^\{-k-1\} (l f' g - k f g') where f and g do not vanish); rankinCohen\_mem\_multiplierSpace: l f' g - k f g' (ModularForm.rankinCohen k l f g) is a modular form in the full sense, in multiplierSpace (k + l + 2) (v \* w), and rankinCohen\_slash is its equivariance under matrices of determinant 1. (8) 'This is a special case of a series of bilinear operators called the Rankin-Cohen operators'. The operators: ModularForm.rankinCohenBracket k l f g n = sum over r + s = n of (-1)\^r C(k+n-1, s) C(l+n-1, r) f\^(r) g\^(s), with generalised binomial coefficients over Z (Ring.choose, so any integers k and l are allowed) and derivatives in z. It is the standard bracket of Cohen 1975 and Zagier 1994 (the same signs and binomial coefficients: compared with the formula on Wikipedia's page 'Rankin-Cohen bracket', which cites both, and with the two computed examples below; the papers themselves could not be read) with d/dz in place of D = (2 pi i)\^-1 d/dz, so (2 pi i)\^n times the bracket in Zagier's normalisation. The special case: rankinCohenBracket\_one, \[f, g\]\_1 = -(l f' g - k f g'), the knowl's operator up to sign (and rankinCohenBracket\_zero, \[f, g\]\_0 = f g). Cohen's theorem, ModularForm.rankinCohenBracket\_mem\_multiplierSpace (file Multiplier/RankinCohen.lean): for every subgroup Gamma of SL(2, R), all integers k and l (negative ones included), all homomorphisms v and w from Gamma to the unit circle and every n, if f is in multiplierSpace k v and g in multiplierSpace l w, then \[f, g\]\_n is in multiplierSpace (k + l + 2n) (v \* w): a modular form in the full sense (holomorphic, the functional equation with multiplier v w, bounded at every cusp of Gamma). It follows from ModularForm.rankinCohenBracket\_slash, \[f, g\]\_n \|\_\{k+l+2n\} gamma = \[f\|\_k gamma, g\|\_l gamma\]\_n for holomorphic f and g and det gamma = 1, which is proved by an algebraic identity in a differential ring (file RingTheory/Derivation/RankinCohen.lean, on Mathlib's class Differential): in A\[X\] with the derivation extending that of A by X' = -X\^2 (Mathlib's Differential.implicitDeriv), the iterated covariant derivatives of u of weight k are polynomials Differential.weightDerivPoly k u r with d/dX of the (r+1)-st equal to (r+1)(k+r) times the r-th (derivative\_weightDerivPoly\_succ); so the bracket of these polynomials, Differential.rankinCohenPoly, has derivative 0 in X (derivative\_rankinCohenPoly, the terms cancel in pairs) and, when A has no additive torsion, takes the same value at every x as at 0, where it is the bracket Differential.rankinCohenBracket of u and v (aeval\_rankinCohenPoly). The holomorphic functions on H form such a ring (ModularForm.holomorphicSubalgebra with f -\> f', an instance of Differential; a C-algebra, so without torsion), x = c/(cz+d) (ModularForm.slashDefect) satisfies x' = -x\^2, and by derivOfComplex\_slash the slashes f\^(r)\|\_\{k+2r\} gamma are the values at x of the polynomials of f\|\_k gamma. Non-vacuity (scratch/AI9c\_rankin\_cohen/Check.lean and Examples.lean): the theorem applies to any two Mathlib modular forms on any Gamma, with a Mathlib modular form of weight k + l + 2n as result, in particular to E\_4 and E\_6 on SL(2, Z) for every n; and from Mathlib's formulas of Ramanujan and Delta = (E\_4\^3 - E\_6\^2)/1728, \[E\_4, E\_6\]\_1 = -(2 pi i) 3456 Delta and \[E\_4, E\_4\]\_2 = (2 pi i)\^2 4800 Delta, which vanish nowhere (in Zagier's normalisation these are -3456 Delta and 4800 Delta, the values that the first q-coefficients give). SECTION 3, changing the group. (9) f(mz): slash\_mem\_multiplierSpace (for det alpha \> 0, f\|\_k alpha is in multiplierSpace k (conjMultiplier v alpha) on alpha\^-1 Gamma alpha, with multiplier delta -\> v(alpha delta alpha\^-1)); for alpha = dilationGL m = diag(m, 1) with m in Q positive, comp\_dilationGL\_smul\_mem\_multiplierSpace (z -\> f(mz) is in that space; coe\_dilationGL\_smul: the point is mz), and alpha\^-1 Gamma alpha is arithmetic if Gamma is (instance, from Mathlib's IsArithmetic.conj). TEXT ERROR 2: mz is in the upper half-plane only for m \> 0. Old: 'for any \$m\\in\\Q\^\{\\times\}\$'; new: 'for any \$m\\in\\Q\_\{\>0\}\$'. (10) Sub-series and twists, for a Mathlib modular form f on Gamma (determinant 1, trivial multiplier), N a strict period of Gamma (so that f = sum a\_n q\^\{n/N\} with q\^\{1/N\} = e\^\{2 pi i z/N\}) and psi a function modulo M: ModularForm.twistOfWidth N psi f = M\^-1 sum over a mod M of (DFT psi)(a) f(z + aN/M), a modular form on twistLevel Gamma M N, the intersection of the conjugates of Gamma by the translations by aN/M (a subgroup of Gamma; it has determinant 1, has N as a strict period, and is arithmetic if Gamma is); qExpansion\_twistOfWidth\_coeff: its n-th coefficient in q\^\{1/N\} is psi(n) a\_n; qExpansion\_twistOfWidth\_single\_coeff: for the indicator function of r mod M the coefficients are a\_n for n = r mod M and 0 otherwise, the sub-series. For the text's n \>= n\_0 with n\_0 \< 0 (poles at the cusp): SlashInvariantForm.twistOfWidth, the same sum of translates for any function invariant under the weight k action of Gamma (it is invariant under twistLevel Gamma M N, and holomorphic if f is: mdifferentiable\_twistOfWidth), and SlashInvariantForm.hasSum\_twistOfWidth: if f = sum over n in Z of a\_n q\^\{n/N\}, then the twist is sum over n in Z of psi(n) a\_n q\^\{n/N\}. Readings: 'modular' with trivial multiplier (for a multiplier of infinite order the claim is false, see review/AI9b\_history\_new.md; for one of finite order restrict to its kernel; on Gamma\_1(N), Tau Ceti's ModularForm.twist\_mem\_modFormCharSpace gives the nebentypus of the twist); for a modular form in the LMFDB's sense n\_0 \>= 0; meromorphy at the other cusps of a form with poles is not stated (no notion at the pins). For Gamma\_1(N) and q = e\^\{2 pi i z\}, Tau Ceti's ModularForm.twist and qExpansion\_twist\_coeff give the same on the explicit group Gamma\_1(L). SECTION 4, enlarging the group. (11) sum\_slash\_eq\_trace: for a finite family gamma\_i in G such that the cosets (H cap G) gamma\_i are the right cosets of H cap G in G, each once, sum\_i f\|\_k gamma\_i is the function of Mathlib's ModularForm.trace G f, a modular form on G. The knowl calls the cosets H gamma\_i left cosets: terminology only. (12) 'special case of the averaging procedure': cross-reference to mf.gl2.history.poincare (ruling of 2026-11-08). (13) Hecke operators. 'combining the above two methods': heckeTNat\_eq\_trace\_translate, Tau Ceti's T\_p on Gamma\_1(N) is the trace of the translate of f by diag(1, p), for modular forms and every p \>= 1 (Tau Ceti has it for cusp forms). At level 1 (Tau Ceti's Gamma\_1(1), which is SL(2, Z) by TauCeti.Gamma1\_one\_map\_eq\_SL), p prime: heckeTNat\_apply\_of\_level\_one, T\_p f (z) = (sum over 0 \<= j \< p of f((z+j)/p) + p\^k f(pz))/p for Tau Ceti's heckeTNat, the standard normalisation, so g = p T\_p f is modular on Gamma. f(pz) is TauCeti.ModularForm.levelRaise p on Gamma\_0(p), through Gamma0\_map\_le\_conjAct\_scaleGL\_one. f((z+j)/p) is HeckeRing.GL2.upperTriTranslate f j (upperTriTranslate\_apply), a modular form on the conjugate (S T\^j)\^-1 Gamma\_0(p) S T\^j: it is p\^k times the translate of f(pz) by S T\^j. TEXT ERROR 3: f((z+j)/p) is not modular on Gamma\_0(p). Proved for every j: qExpansion\_coeff\_one\_eq\_zero\_of\_slash\_upperTriTranslate (if z -\> f((z+j)/p) is invariant under T, which lies in Gamma\_0(p), then a\_1(f) = 0) and not\_forall\_slash\_upperTriTranslate\_discriminant (so Delta((z+j)/p) is not modular on Gamma\_0(p), nor on Gamma). Old: 'will be modular only on the subgroup \{\{KNOWL('group.sl2z.subgroup.gamma0n', "\$\\Gamma\_0(p)\$")\}\} of \$\\Gamma\$'; new: 'will be modular only on conjugates of the subgroup \{\{KNOWL('group.sl2z.subgroup.gamma0n', "\$\\Gamma\_0(p)\$")\}\} of \$\\Gamma\$'. 'Only' for f(pz): not\_slash\_levelRaise\_discriminant, Delta(pz) is not invariant under S, so f(pz) is not modular on Gamma in general. Not linked (eight-declaration cap): mul\_mem\_multiplierSpace and rankinCohen\_mem\_multiplierSpace (linked in AI9b's record; they are the cases n = 0 and n = 1 of the linked Cohen theorem, through rankinCohenBracket\_zero and rankinCohenBracket\_one, and stay proved directly); Mathlib's ModularForm.instGCommRing, ModularForm.trace and Derivative.serreDerivativeMF; Tau Ceti's TauCeti.ModularForm.levelRaise, HeckeRing.GL2.heckeTNat and ModularForm.twist; from the four files, apply\_smul\_of\_mem\_multiplierSpace, hasDerivAt\_smul\_coe, derivOfComplex\_slash, eq\_zero\_of\_derivOfComplex\_smul, derivOfComplex\_mem\_multiplierSpace, maassShimura\_slash, serreDerivative\_mem\_multiplierSpace, zpow\_div\_zpow\_slash, derivOfComplex\_zpow\_div\_zpow\_slash, derivOfComplex\_zpow\_div\_zpow, rankinCohenBracket\_zero, rankinCohenBracket\_one, rankinCohenBracket\_slash, Differential.aeval\_rankinCohenPoly, slash\_mem\_multiplierSpace, coe\_dilationGL\_smul, comp\_dilationGL\_smul\_mem\_multiplierSpace, qExpansion\_twistOfWidth\_single\_coeff, SlashInvariantForm.hasSum\_twistOfWidth, upperTriTranslate\_apply, heckeTNat\_eq\_trace\_translate and the two Delta counterexamples. Checks. By the 