* [`mf.gl2.history.poincare`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Modular-forms/Modular-forms-miscellaneous/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_mf___gl2___history___poincare_FLQQ_--statement) — Poincar&eacute; series. Declarations (LeanBridge): `ModularForm.averageSeries`, `ModularForm.averageSeries_smul`, `ModularForm.summable_norm_averageTerm`, `ModularForm.averageSeries_one_eq_E`, `ModularForm.cexp_smul_T`, `ModularForm.averageSeries_cexp`, `ModularForm.summable_norm_averageTerm_zpow`, `ModularForm.fullAverageSeries_eq_averageSeries`. Note: Round AI (AI5\_mf\_history), reviewed (review/AI5\_mf\_history.md). Audit record N4.json: 'Partially in LeanBridge', medium. All of the knowl's mathematics is proved for Gamma = SL\_2(Z) (link group.sl2z) and integral weight k, sentence by sentence. (1) 'We can define a function which has the modular transformation property of modular forms by averaging over the group action: f(z) = sum over \<T\>\\Gamma of h(gamma z)(cz+d)\^-k, whenever this makes sense'. ModularForm.averageSeries is the tsum (0 when not summable) over the right cosets \<T\> gamma, with chosen representatives; for 1-periodic h the terms depend only on the coset (averageTerm\_eq\_of\_rightRel). averageSeries\_smul is the transformation property f(gamma z) = (cz+d)\^k f(z) for every gamma in SL\_2(Z) and every 1-periodic h; it holds for the tsum, so wherever the series converges absolutely, and trivially (0 = 0) elsewhere. 'Makes sense': summable\_norm\_averageTerm (added by the review) proves absolute convergence in weight k \>= 3 whenever h is bounded on the orbit of z, in particular for bounded h. averageSeries\_eq\_zero\_of\_odd (added by the review): in odd weight the average is 0, the terms at gamma and -gamma cancelling; the trivial multiplier is a multiplier system only for even k. (2) 'If h = 1 we recover the Eisenstein series'. averageSeries\_one: the bottom row identifies \<T\>\\Gamma with the coprime pairs, so the average is Mathlib's level-1 eisensteinSeries, the sum over coprime (c, d) of (cz+d)\^-k. averageSeries\_one\_eq\_E: that is 2 E\_k (Mathlib's normalised E, k \>= 3). NORMALISATION, a question for the editors and not an error of substance: \<T\> does not contain -I, while cmf.eisenstein sums over Gamma\_infty = +-\<T\>, so h = 1 gives twice the linked E\_k. Old: 'If \$h=1\$ we recover the \{\{KNOWL('cmf.eisenstein', "Eisenstein series")\}\}.'; new: 'If \$h=1\$ we recover twice the \{\{KNOWL('cmf.eisenstein', "Eisenstein series")\}\} \$E\_k\$.' (3) 'If h(z) = exp(2 pi i n z) with n in Z, which is indeed 1-periodic, we produce what are called Poincare series'. cexp\_smul\_T: e\^\{2 pi i n z\} is invariant under T for every n in Z. averageSeries\_cexp: in even weight k \>= 3 and for every n in Z, the average is twice the Poincare series P\_k\^n(Gamma, 1; z; infinity) of specialfunction.poincare, i.e. LeanBridge's poincareSeries with G = top, gamma\_s = 1, v = 1, w = 1; absolute convergence for every n in Z from summable\_norm\_averageTerm and Mathlib's exists\_max\_im. The factor 2 is the same normalisation; for odd k, specialfunction.poincare's condition v(-I) = (-1)\^k excludes v = 1. (4) 'A special case for which it is not necessary to divide by the left-action of T is the function f(z) = sum over gamma in Gamma of (gamma z)\^-n (cz+d)\^-k, for suitable values of n'. fullAverageSeries is that sum over all of SL\_2(Z). summable\_norm\_averageTerm\_zpow gives absolute convergence for 2 \<= n \<= k - 2, the full range of absolute convergence and the range of the knowl's source (H. Cohen, An introduction to modular forms, arXiv:1809.10907, the exercise on phi(tau) = tau\^-m: 2 \<= m \<= k - 2). The author proved 3 \<= n \<= k - 3 only (empty for k = 4, 5); the review replaced the proof: where \|az+b\| \<= \|cz+d\|, gamma is determined by its top row and the integer m nearest to Re((cz+d)/(az+b)), and \|cz+d\| \>= \|az+b\| \|m+i\| / 3, so the term is at most 3\^\{k-n\} \|az+b\|\^-k \|m+i\|\^-(k-n); elsewhere the same for S gamma. fullAverageSeries\_smul is the transformation property (it also follows from the next two and averageSeries\_smul). tsum\_zpow\_smul\_T shows w -\> sum over m in Z of (w+m)\^-n is 1-periodic. fullAverageSeries\_eq\_averageSeries (now for 2 \<= n \<= k - 2): the sum over Gamma is the average of that function over \<T\>\\Gamma, via the bijection (\<T\>\\Gamma) x Z = SL\_2(Z), (q, m) -\> T\^m q.out. So it is a special case of the construction. not\_summable\_averageTerm\_one (renamed from not\_summable\_denom\_zpow): for h = 1 the sum over all of Gamma diverges (the terms at T\^m are 1), which is why dividing by T is necessary in general. Not linked (eight-declaration cap; the review linked summable\_norm\_averageTerm in place of fullAverageSeries\_smul): averageTerm, averageSeries\_one, averageSeries\_eq\_zero\_of\_odd, fullAverageSeries, fullAverageSeries\_smul, tsum\_zpow\_smul\_T, not\_summable\_averageTerm\_one, and the existing ModularForm.poincareSeries and Mathlib eisensteinSeries/E. Checks by the review: lake build through the guard; \#lint: 0 errors; \#print axioms: propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound; dup\_decls: 0 clashes; all public names new in Mathlib, Tau Ceti (pin and main) and LeanBridge.
