# Representations › Mod-ℓ Galois

* [`modlgal`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Representations/Mod-l-Galois/#--informal-preview-modlgal--statement) — Mod-\$\\ell\$ Galois representation. Declarations (LeanBridge · Mathlib): `MonoidHom.IsModEllGaloisRepIn`, `AlgebraicGeometry.rationalPoints`, `Field.absoluteGaloisGroup`, `MonoidHom.isModEllGaloisRepIn_one`. Note: The GL\_n restriction is lifted: IsModEllGaloisRepIn is a homomorphism ρ : Gal(K̄/K) → G(𝔽\_ℓ) with open kernel (continuity for the discrete topology), ℓ prime, for an algebraic group G over 𝔽\_ℓ taken as a group scheme of finite type over 𝔽\_ℓ (a group object G of Over (Spec 𝔽\_ℓ), as in Tau Ceti's GroupScheme files, with quasi-compact and locally finite type structure morphism; Milne, Algebraic Groups, Def. 1.1); G(𝔽\_ℓ) = rationalPoints G, the sections 𝟙 ⟶ G over Spec 𝔽\_ℓ with the group law of the group object (Mathlib's MonObj.Hom.group). Non-vacuity: isModEllGaloisRepIn\_one (Tau Ceti's GL\_n group scheme over 𝔽\_ℓ, the trivial representation). Review (ZZ7): outcome changed to partial. The reading is not a model (no presentation or embedding is chosen), but it is a convention the knowl does not fix: ag.algebraic\_group is TBA, and standard texts differ (Milne, Algebraic Groups 2017: group schemes of finite type; Milne's notes on affine group schemes and Waterhouse: affine group schemes of finite type; Borel, Humphreys, Springer: smooth group varieties). The Lean predicate takes the broadest, so it is the knowl's notion under Milne's reading and strictly broader under the others (it admits non-reduced G such as μ\_ℓ over 𝔽\_ℓ, and non-affine G such as abelian varieties); by the rulings of 2026-11-04 (a convention the knowl does not fix; when in doubt, partial) the outcome is partial. If the user accepts Milne's definition as the knowl's meaning, the knowl is formalised as it stands, with no further Lean work. Blocked by: the LMFDB knowl ag.algebraic\_group (TBA), which would fix what 'algebraic group' means. The existing GL\_n notion (IsModEllGaloisRep) is the case G = GL\_n up to Tau Ceti's identification of GL\_n's points over Spec 𝔽\_ℓ with GL\_n(𝔽\_ℓ) (schemePointsMulEquiv); a formal MulEquiv for the terminal-object points was dropped because the kernel spends about 3 minutes checking the isomorphism (Spec 𝔽\_ℓ).asOver (Spec 𝔽\_ℓ) ≅ 𝟙\_. Text error, pure typo (does not by itself keep the knowl partial, ruling of 2026-11-07): old "abolute Galois group", new "absolute Galois group" (once in the live knowl, inside \{\{ KNOWL('group.galois.absolute', ...) \}\}). Ruling of 2026-11-08: the user accepts Milne's definition (an algebraic group over 𝔽\_ℓ is a group scheme of finite type), so the outcome is formalised as it stands.
* [`modlgal.codomain`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Representations/Mod-l-Galois/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_modlgal___codomain_FLQQ_--statement) — Codomain. Declarations (LeanBridge): `AlgebraicGeometry.rationalPoints`, `MonoidHom.IsModEllGaloisRepIn`, `MonoidHom.IsModEllGaloisRepIn.finite_points`, `AlgebraicGeometry.finite_points_of_finiteType`. Note: The codomain of ρ : Gal(K̄/K) → G(𝔽\_ℓ) is rationalPoints G, the group of 𝔽\_ℓ-points of the algebraic group G (group scheme of finite type over 𝔽\_ℓ, see the modlgal record), for every such G, not only GL\_n. Its finiteness, stated by the knowl, is proved: IsModEllGaloisRepIn.finite\_points, from the new general theorem finite\_points\_of\_finiteType (a scheme of finite type over a finite field k has finitely many k-points: each point factors through one of the finitely many affine opens Spec A of a finite subcover, where it is a k-algebra map A → k by Tau Ceti's pointsEquivAlgHom, with A finitely generated; the file adds the instance Finite (A →ₐ\[R\] B) for A of finite type and B finite, not in Mathlib or Tau Ceti at the pins, where Mathlib has it only for module-finite algebras into a field or free ones into a domain). Review (ZZ7): stays formalised although modlgal is partial, because neither the codomain nor its finiteness depends on the reading of 'algebraic group': rationalPoints G is the group of 𝔽\_ℓ-points under every standard definition, and finite\_points is proved for every group scheme of finite type over 𝔽\_ℓ, so for an algebraic group in any of the standard senses (all are of finite type; a reading beyond finite type would make the knowl's 'finite group' false). If the user prefers to treat the three modlgal knowls alike, this one is partial with modlgal's blocker (ag.algebraic\_group TBA). Every sentence is captured; no text error.
