evel\_subset\_iff); \[H, H\] is open in SL2(Zhat) (adelicSL\_inf\_ker\_le\_commutator: it contains the elements of SL2(Zhat) = 1 mod (2M)\^4 for H containing the kernel mod M, each a product of five commutators at every prime, uniformly, by ElementaryCommutator.exists\_eq\_prod\_commutatorElement over local rings); \[𝒢, 𝒢\] = \[H, H\] modulo the elements trivial at P with central scalars (commutator\_adelicScalars\_sup\_sup\_adelicAwayFrom); 𝒢 is agreeable since SL2(Z\_l) at an odd l is generated by unipotents (mem\_closure\_range\_upperRightHom\_union\_range\_lowerUnipotent), each a commutator with an element of 𝒢 of component diag(2,1) or diag(1,2) at l (mulSingle\_mem\_commutator, using det(𝒢) = Zhat\^x); minimality (adelicScalars\_sup\_sup\_adelicAwayFrom\_le) uses only that H is open. Text error (recorded for the editors; it does not affect the outcome under the ruling of 2026-11-12): the knowl states the claims for every open H, but without det(H) = Zhat\^x they fail: H = \{g : det(g\_3) = 1 mod 3\} (LeanBridge's detModThreeKer, index 2) has H\~ = GL2(Zhat) with \[H\~, H\~\] containing SL2(Z\_3) while \[H, H\] reduces at 3 into \[SL2(F\_3), SL2(F\_3)\] = Q\_8; and for H the inverse image at 7 of a subgroup C ≅ SL2(F\_3) of SL2(F\_7), H\~ = GL2(Zhat) but H is not normal in it (brute force in GL2(F\_7)). Review (AG4): formalised confirmed; the upper unipotent matrices are now Mathlib's upperRightHom (the new upperUnipotent was the same term); the conjunction lemmas adelicLevel\_mem and adelicLevel\_pos\_and\_ker\_le split, the latter's second half being the committed PSL2Index.ker\_adelicReduction\_adelicLevel\_le, now used; theorem -\> lemma; binders hoisted; stale docstrings of the committed Agreeable.lean listed in the review.
