* [`sh.disc`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Varieties/Shimura-curves/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_sh___disc_FLQQ_--statement) — Discriminant of a Shimura curve. Declarations (LeanBridge): `QuaternionAlgebra.discriminant`, `QuaternionAlgebra.IsRamifiedAt`, `QuaternionAlgebra.finite_setOf_isRamifiedAt`, `QuaternionAlgebra.discriminant_eq_prod`, `PolarizedQuaternionOrder.shimuraCurveLabel`. Note: Round AJ (AJ3): both tracker blockers are stale. (1) The finiteness of the set of ramified primes, the gap of shimcurve.discb named by the blocker, was closed in round ZZ7: QuaternionAlgebra.finite\_setOf\_isRamifiedAt (a quaternion algebra over Q, central simple of dimension 4, is ramified at finitely many primes) and QuaternionAlgebra.discriminant\_eq\_prod (disc(B), the committed finprod, is the finite product of the ramified primes); shimcurve.discb is formalised. (2) 'Shimura curves themselves (shimcurve)': for modular curves, modcurve.level ('The level of a modular curve X\_H is the level of the open subgroup H') is formalised on the group H although X\_H is not formalised, while modcurve.index is partial only for its second sentence, a theorem about the curve (the degree of j : X\_H -\> X(1)). sh.disc is the modcurve.level case: it defines an invariant of the curve as an invariant of the data the curve is built from, the quaternion algebra B of the polarized order (O, mu) (PolarizedQuaternionOrder B), and states no theorem about the curve. The label records it in this way: the discriminant entry of PolarizedQuaternionOrder.shimuraCurveLabel is QuaternionAlgebra.discriminant B. So the knowl is formalised by the declarations of shimcurve.discb; no new Lean. Every sentence is captured; no text error. Review (AJ3): confirmed. The one sentence defines the discriminant of a Shimura curve through its data, the algebra B (the entry D of shimuraCurveLabel), as modcurve.level defines the level of X\_H through H and shimcurve.label's D.d.δ.N.i are formalised on the data; it states nothing about the curve (no degree, map or moduli property). The five decls exist in the modules given.
