* [`shimcurve.disco`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Varieties/Shimura-curves/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_shimcurve___disco_FLQQ_--statement) — Discriminant of a quaternion order. Declarations (LeanBridge): `Subalgebra.quaternionDisc`, `Subalgebra.quaternionDiscrd`, `Subalgebra.quaternionDisc_eq_quaternionDiscOfBasis`, `Subalgebra.exists_quaternionDiscrd_eq_natCast`, `Subalgebra.quaternionDiscrd_pos`, `Subalgebra.quaternionDiscrd_sq`. Note: All the stated facts are now proved, for an order O of a quaternion algebra B over ℚ (central simple, dimension 4): O has a ℤ-basis of four elements (nonempty\_basis\_of\_isQuaternionOrder; free of rank 4, its ℤ-bases are ℚ-bases of B by Mathlib's extendOfIsLattice), so the committed quaternionDisc never takes its junk value; disc(O) does not depend on the basis (quaternionDisc\_eq\_quaternionDiscOfBasis: a change of ℤ-basis multiplies the determinant by (det P)² = 1); disc(O) is an integer (exists\_quaternionDisc\_eq\_natCast, via exists\_reducedTrace\_eq\_intCast: trd is integral on O, since for x ∈ O integral over ℤ the minimal polynomial X² - trd(x) X + nrd(x) over ℚ divides a monic integer polynomial and so has integer coefficients by Gauss's lemma); and discrd(O) = Rat.sqrt disc(O) is a positive integer whose square is disc(O) (exists\_quaternionDiscrd\_eq\_natCast, quaternionDiscrd\_pos, quaternionDiscrd\_sq). The square comes from B ≅ ℍ\[ℚ,a,b\]: for any ℚ-basis y, det(trd(y\_i y\_j)) = -(4ab·det Q)² with Q the matrix of y in 1, i, j, k, where the trace form is diag(2, 2a, 2b, -2ab) (det\_toMatrix\_traceForm), so disc(O) is the square of a rational number whose square is an integer, hence of an integer, nonzero as a, b ≠ 0. trd is the committed reducedTrace (half the trace of left multiplication), shown invariant under algebra isomorphisms (reducedTrace\_algEquiv) and equal to x + x̄ on ℍ\[ℚ,a,b\] (committed), so it is the reduced trace. No Voight-style alternating form is needed. exists\_quaternionDisc\_eq\_natCast (disc(O) is an integer, as the knowl states) is left out of decls only by the cap of six; it also follows at once from exists\_quaternionDiscrd\_eq\_natCast and quaternionDiscrd\_sq. The committed QuaternionOrder.lean docstrings still say these facts are not proved there; replacement text is in review/ZZ7\_forms\_quaternions.md. Every sentence is captured. Text error, pure typo (formalised by the ruling of 2026-11-07): a full stop before 'where' inside the display; old "\\left\|\\det(\\operatorname\{trd\}(x\_ix\_j))\_\{i,j\}\\right\|. \\\]", new "\\left\|\\det(\\operatorname\{trd\}(x\_ix\_j))\_\{i,j\}\\right\|, \\\]".
* [`shimcurve.enhanced_group`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Varieties/Shimura-curves/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_shimcurve___enhanced_group_FLQQ_--statement) — Enhanced group associated to a polarized quaternion order. Declarations (LeanBridge): `Subalgebra.completedUnits`, `Subalgebra.completedUnitsTopology`, `Subalgebra.completedUnitsProfinite`, `Subalgebra.compactSpace_completedUnits`, `Subalgebra.completedUnitsAction`, `Subalgebra.EnhancedGroup`. Note: Ô\^× = lim\_N (O/NO)\^× (compatible families, completedUnits), its identification with the units of Ô = O ⊗ ℤ̂ (committed: completedUnitsEquivUnits and the O ⊗ ℤ̂ comparison of FiniteAdelicComparison), the action of Aut\_\{±μ\}(O) and the semidirect product were formalised; the gap, 'Ô\^× is a profinite group', is now proved. completedUnitsTopology is the inverse limit topology (subspace of ∏\_N (O/NO)\^× with discrete factors), for which the reductions Ô\^× → (O/NO)\^× are continuous (continuous\_completedUnitsReduction), Ô\^× is a topological group (isTopologicalGroup\_completedUnits), compact when O is a finitely generated ℤ-module, e.g. an order (compactSpace\_completedUnits: closed in a product of finite spaces, O/NO being finite by Mathlib's finite\_of\_fg\_torsion), totally disconnected and Hausdorff; completedUnitsProfinite packages Ô\^× as a Mathlib ProfiniteGrp for every order O. The topology is a definition, not a global instance, to avoid a clash with a subspace topology if B carried one. Non-vacuity: isQuaternionOrder\_range\_mapMatrix shows M₂(ℤ) is an order of M₂(ℚ) (no concrete order existed in LeanBridge). The knowl puts no topology on the enhanced group itself, and none is added. Every sentence is captured; no text error.
* [`shimcurve.index`](https://cbirkbeck.github.io/LeanBridge/Varieties/Shimura-curves/#--informal-preview-_FLQQ_shimcurve___index_FLQQ_--statement) — Index of a Shimura curve. Declarations (Mathlib · LeanBridge): `Subgroup.index`, `Subalgebra.EnhancedGroup`, `Subalgebra.enhancedGroupTopology`, `Subalgebra.isTopologicalGroup_enhancedGroup`, `Subalgebra.isOpen_enhancedGroupKernel`, `Subalgebra.isCompact_enhancedGroupKernel`, `PolarizedQuaternionOrder.isCompact_of_isOpen`, `PolarizedQuaternionOrder.finiteIndex_of_isOpen`. Note: Round AJ (AJ3). The blockers were the profinite topology of the enhanced group, to restrict to open compact H, and the Shimura curves X\_H. Round ZZ7 gave the topology of O-hat\^x only (completedUnitsTopology, completedUnitsProfinite); the knowl's 'open compact subgroup H \<= Aut\_\{+-mu\}(O) x\| O-hat\^x' needs one on the enhanced group. New: EnhancedGroupTopology.lean gives the enhanced group the product of the profinite topology of O-hat\^x and the discrete topology of Aut\_\{+-mu\}(O) (Subalgebra.enhancedGroupTopology, a def like completedUnitsTopology, the topology for which O-hat\^x is an open subgroup with its profinite topology); with it the enhanced group is a topological group (isTopologicalGroup\_enhancedGroup), the kernels Gamma(O-hat, N) of O-hat\^x -\> (O/NO)\^x are open (isOpen\_enhancedGroupKernel) and compact for an order (isCompact\_enhancedGroupKernel), so open compact subgroups exist, and every open subgroup contains one of them (exists\_enhancedGroupKernel\_le\_of\_isOpen). AutPmMuFinite.lean proves that Aut\_\{+-mu\}(O) is finite for every order O of a quaternion algebra over Q and every mu with mu\^2 a negative rational (Subalgebra.finite\_autPmMu): an element gamma of N(O) commuting with mu lies in Q + Q mu (QuaternionTraceNorm.lean, eq\_algebraMap\_add\_smul\_of\_mul\_comm), conjugation by gamma preserves the lattice O so its trace trd(gamma)\^2/nrd(gamma) = 4s\^2/(s\^2 - m t\^2) is an integer c (exists\_sq\_reducedTrace\_div\_eq\_intCast), in \{0, 1, 2, 3\} when t != 0, and (4 - c)(s/t)\^2 = -cm leaves finitely many classes modulo Q\^x; the elements with gamma\^-1 mu gamma = -mu form at most one more coset. So for a polarized order (PolarizedQuaternionOrder.finite\_autPmMu) the enhanced group is compact (PolarizedQuaternionOrder.compactSpace\_enhancedGroup), its open subgroups are exactly its open compact subgroups (PolarizedQuaternionOrder.isCompact\_of\_isOpen) and have finite index (PolarizedQuaternionOrder.finiteIndex\_of\_isOpen), so the index, Mathlib's Subgroup.index of H, is a positive integer. 'Shimura curves themselves': the knowl defines the index of X\_H as the index of H and states no theorem about the curve; this is the modcurve.level case (formalised on the group H, X\_H not being formalised), not the modcurve.index case (partial for its theorem on the degree of j : X\_H -\> X(1)). Proposed formalised. Every sentence is captured; no text error. Review (AJ3): confirmed. The index is the index of H in the enhanced group, as the knowl says ('in the enhanced semidirect product'), and it is finite for open H, so never Mathlib's junk value 0. The topology is the only reasonable one: a Hausdorff group topology inducing the profinite topology on the finite-index subgroup O-hat\^x makes it compact, hence closed, hence open, hence gives this product topology. Subalgebra.finite\_autPmMu checked step by step (centraliser Q + Q mu, integral trace 4s\^2/(s\^2 - m t\^2) of conjugation, at most one extra coset). Non-vacuity: every hypothesis of the Subalgebra results is met by M\_2(Z) in M\_2(Q) with mu = \[\[0, -1\], \[1, 0\]\] (scratch AJ3\_shimura\_orders-review/NonVacuity.lean); LeanBridge has no concrete PolarizedQuaternionOrder (it needs an indefinite division algebra), so the PolarizedQuaternionOrder corollaries are checked only through their Subalgebra versions; B = (-1, 3 / Q), O = Z + Zi + Zj + Zij, mu = i would give one in about 150 lines (a follow-up, not a gap of this 